ent — this is the one place an asymmetry would invalidate the comparison.\n- Secondary: total prompt+completion tokens across all calls per query, and LLM-call count per query. These are the fallback primary metrics if a stable shared endpoint cannot be guaranteed, so make the design degrade gracefully to them.\n- Keep the LLM-time vs overhead decomposition; it is what lets a reader attribute an increase to structural cost (extra calls/tokens) rather than serving noise.\n\nCONTROLS TO KEEP (and one to demote)\n- Keep: identical model/endpoint/decoding config (temperature, max_tokens, stop), pinned serving version, warm-up trials excluded, randomized/interleaved condition order per query to defeat prefix-cache and load drift, R repeats per cell with median + bootstrap CI, outlier-block discard rule stated in advance.\n- Keep the meta-buffer frozen at a fixed pre-built size during measurement, and report cold/growing-buffer behavior as a separately characterized variant rather than mixing it into the primary comparison.\n- Demote the empty-buffer BoT arm from a co-primary condition to an optional diagnostic ablation, so scope stays on the question asked.\n- Accuracy is contextual only: include a small accuracy-vs-latency table so added time can be interpreted, but do not let it become a second headline.\n\nANALYSIS PLAN — make it concrete, not just named\n- State the estimand: paired per-query latency difference (and log-ratio) BoT vs Expert, and BoT vs PAL, per benchmark, then pooled.\n- Give the one-sided test, the effect-size summary you will report (median paired difference + bootstrap CI, relative overhead %), the condition x benchmark interaction check, and how you would handle multiplicity across the two comparisons x three benchmarks. For the pooled model, specify fixed effects and random intercepts, and what you would report if the mixed model fails to converge on real data.\n- Pre-register a practical-significance framing: define in advance what relative ove